WEBVTT

NOTE
Generated by scripts/song-video/render.js from the song storyboard in _data/songs/; edit that file, not this one.

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.280
(narrator) Audio-described version: before each section, the picture pauses while I describe it.

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:16.340
(narrator) The song is a comic opera on the stage of an opera house dressed as a software team's office: red velvet drapes, a gilded arch, footlights, and a conductor in the pit.

3
00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:27.620
(narrator) On the back wall are an arched window onto the city, a wall clock beside it showing 9:30 a.m., and a board headed Build status with six lamps, all green with ticks.

4
00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:44.740
(narrator) The curtain rises, and a follow spot picks up the Teammate, the tenor, gliding in from the left: a towering brown pompadour, a twirled moustache, a teal quarter-zip pullover over a white tee, studio headphones round his neck, jeans, and orange sneakers.

5
00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:51.000
(narrator) An empty desk with a tapered metal paper bin beside it stands on the left and stays throughout the opera.

6
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:56.160
(narrator) At a desk on the right, your hero, or the default Bruin, types at a laptop.

7
00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.620
(narrator) He raises a calming palm to them and pats his chest.

8
00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:04.100
(narrator) A sprint calendar sheds its fourteen days down to: ship today.

9
00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:11.880
(narrator) A toy ship flagged v1.0 by 3:00 is flown in from the rafters on two wires, and he salutes it.

10
00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:16.900
(narrator) Just before the Product Manager enters, the clock advances to three in the afternoon.

11
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:26.760
(narrator) Each teammate who answers him later is a woman with a face of her own, chosen at random and kept all song; at the very end your hero joins them.

12
00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.307
[music]

13
00:01:29.307 --> 00:01:34.937
♪ (Teammate) No need to worry! Just leave it to me! ♪

14
00:01:35.487 --> 00:01:42.197
♪ I'll save us a whole sprint— We'll ship it by three! ♪

15
00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:45.340
(narrator) Act one, Requirements.

16
00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:51.856
(narrator) A long scroll of requirements unrolls behind the Teammate, and he snaps it shut with one raised finger.

17
00:01:52.056 --> 00:02:01.516
(narrator) A giant phone running a ticket app, SeatSwap, shows a concert ticket, then one big Buy button, then the ticket locked to one name, gleaming.

18
00:02:01.716 --> 00:02:07.296
(narrator) Silhouetted hands in the audience go up with question marks; he shushes them and taps his temple.

19
00:02:07.496 --> 00:02:14.296
(narrator) A card for a meeting with users, What do they need?, appears, and he tears it in two and tosses the halves away.

20
00:02:14.496 --> 00:02:16.816
(narrator) The torn card leaves with this scene.

21
00:02:17.016 --> 00:02:25.356
(narrator) He bows to a flashing Applause sign, but the house stays silent, and two thought bubbles swoop up from the stalls: Not what I need!

22
00:02:25.556 --> 00:02:27.056
(narrator) Nobody asked us!

23
00:02:27.653 --> 00:02:31.753
♪ Requirements? Requirements? Quite simple! Tickets: buy and go! ♪

24
00:02:31.753 --> 00:02:34.933
♪ No need to ask users— I'm certain I know! ♪

25
00:02:34.933 --> 00:02:37.713
♪ One ticket! One name! How sleek! How refined! ♪

26
00:02:37.713 --> 00:02:42.300
♪ I'll save them a meeting— They'll thank me in time! ♪

27
00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:46.720
(narrator) Just before the Product Manager enters, the clock reaches 3:00 p.m.

28
00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:58.600
(narrator) The Product Manager glides in from the right, with a woman's face of her own, a navy blazer over a coral tee, a lanyard and badge, grey trousers, white sneakers, and a clipboard.

29
00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.440
(narrator) The Teammate freezes with a sheepish grin and a bead of sweat while they sing.

30
00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:14.140
(narrator) A gold card headed User story writes itself out, part by part: As a fan, I want to pass my ticket on, so a friend can use my seat when I can't come along.

31
00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:16.400
(narrator) The phone answers: Transfer?

32
00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:17.500
(narrator) Not allowed.

33
00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:18.980
(narrator) One name only.

34
00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:23.880
(narrator) The Product Manager shows a card: Demo today, send a ticket to a friend.

35
00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:30.060
(narrator) The Product Manager swings and throws the user-story clipboard already held in their left hand toward the bin.

36
00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:37.320
(narrator) It arcs from the emptying hand into the bin and settles at an angle, protruding above the rim, where it stays.

37
00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:48.700
(narrator) Then the phone fans out into six small phones, red, orange, gold, green, blue, and violet, each with the same one button, and the Product Manager facepalms.

38
00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:53.400
(narrator) His cheeks slowly gain a faint blush as the wrong product is revealed.

39
00:03:53.600 --> 00:04:00.320
(narrator) A brief label above their head identifies the Product Manager; a matching list sticker stays attached to their blazer.

40
00:04:00.897 --> 00:04:04.287
♪ (Product Manager) As a fan, I want To pass my ticket on, ♪

41
00:04:04.287 --> 00:04:07.947
♪ So a friend can use my seat When I can't come along! ♪

42
00:04:07.947 --> 00:04:11.287
♪ I promised a demo Of what they could use. ♪

43
00:04:11.287 --> 00:04:15.600
♪ You've built the wrong product— In six lovely hues! ♪

44
00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:17.820
(narrator) Act two, Reuse.

45
00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:27.060
(narrator) A wall shelf of package boxes slides in: auth, charts, transfers, emoji, dates, and payments, each with its version number.

46
00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:32.780
(narrator) A sticker says Reuse!, and a red card says Build it anew: no, thanks.

47
00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:40.420
(narrator) The transfers package, version 1.4.2, flies off the shelf into the phone, which now sends a ticket to Sam.

48
00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:58.380
(narrator) He flicks away a padlocked document, package-lock.json, and new versions of the transfers package, 1.5.0, 2.0.0, and 2.1.0, march in from the right on little legs, each getting a sticker: fixes!

49
00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:01.380
(narrator) He holds a gold trophy high, beaming.

50
00:05:01.967 --> 00:05:06.587
♪ (Teammate) Transfers? Quite simple! I'll borrow the code! ♪

51
00:05:06.767 --> 00:05:10.097
♪ Design with reuse! Why build it anew? ♪

52
00:05:10.097 --> 00:05:13.027
♪ A package for transfers Will know what to do! ♪

53
00:05:13.027 --> 00:05:16.267
♪ No lockfile to fuss with! Let new versions in! ♪

54
00:05:16.267 --> 00:05:19.333
♪ New versions mean fixes— A sensible win! ♪

55
00:05:19.633 --> 00:05:30.093
(narrator) The Build Engineer glides in from the right, with a woman's face of her own, a hi-vis safety vest over a navy tee, olive cargo trousers, work boots, and a wrench.

56
00:05:30.293 --> 00:05:32.673
(narrator) The Teammate freezes again, sweating.

57
00:05:32.873 --> 00:05:42.633
(narrator) His laptop keeps the old transfers package, 1.4.2, while a blue build machine marked Clean build takes in version 2.1.0.

58
00:05:42.833 --> 00:05:51.833
(narrator) On new, the old transfers 1.4.2 package drops out of the laptop picture and falls to the floor behind the main performance area.

59
00:05:52.033 --> 00:05:58.613
(narrator) It lands on one corner with a slight dent and crease, rocks flat, and stays there with its label visible.

60
00:05:58.813 --> 00:06:06.553
(narrator) A plug for the old call, version one, bounces off a socket for version two's new call, with a spark and a red cross.

61
00:06:06.753 --> 00:06:18.493
(narrator) A lockfile card pins the old version, the first lamp on the Build status board turns red with a cross, his cheeks blush a little more, and a sticker says All builds failed.

62
00:06:18.693 --> 00:06:28.373
(narrator) Last, his laptop drops into a cardboard box marked Overnight, a label slaps on, Fragile: works on my machine, and he clutches his head.

63
00:06:28.573 --> 00:06:33.753
(narrator) The old transfers package remains on the floor, and the discarded clipboard stays in the bin.

64
00:06:33.953 --> 00:06:40.613
(narrator) A brief label above their head identifies the Build Engineer; a matching gear sticker stays attached to their vest.

65
00:06:41.227 --> 00:06:44.727
♪ (Build Engineer) Your laptop kept old ones; My clean build got new. ♪

66
00:06:44.727 --> 00:06:48.427
♪ Version two changed the API— Your calls won't go through! ♪

67
00:06:48.427 --> 00:06:52.227
♪ Next time, pin your versions! Till then, the builds fail. ♪

68
00:06:52.227 --> 00:06:57.433
♪ Shall I ship them your laptop By overnight mail? ♪

69
00:06:57.733 --> 00:06:58.553
(narrator) Refrain.

70
00:06:58.753 --> 00:07:03.393
(narrator) The Teammate stands by his desk, where his laptop shows All tests pass.

71
00:07:03.593 --> 00:07:10.433
(narrator) He lifts it high on one palm in a golden light while the build status board retains one red failed check.

72
00:07:10.633 --> 00:07:15.953
(narrator) Back on the desk, it shows his code in a gilded frame, and he blows a chef's kiss.

73
00:07:16.153 --> 00:07:21.973
(narrator) A flip counter on the desk, Tiny fixes, counts 1, then 5, then 6.

74
00:07:22.173 --> 00:07:32.873
(narrator) Last, six lamps on his laptop's screen turn green, the footlights turn green, and he throws his arms up, while the build status board retains one red failed check.

75
00:07:33.483 --> 00:07:35.293
♪ (Teammate) It works on my machine! ♪

76
00:07:35.293 --> 00:07:39.753
♪ The finest code you've seen! ♪

77
00:07:39.753 --> 00:07:43.083
♪ One tiny fix— Or five or six— ♪

78
00:07:43.083 --> 00:07:45.903
♪ And all the lights turn green! ♪

79
00:07:46.933 --> 00:07:48.573
(narrator) Act three, Testing.

80
00:07:48.773 --> 00:07:57.213
(narrator) A code card, transfer.py, holds a function that checks the owner, gives the seat to a friend, notifies the friend, and logs it.

81
00:07:57.413 --> 00:08:02.973
(narrator) Gold test tags pin onto every line, and green numbers 1 to 4 onto their order.

82
00:08:03.173 --> 00:08:09.713
(narrator) A second card, test_transfer.py, asserts that the helpers are called in exactly that order.

83
00:08:09.913 --> 00:08:13.453
(narrator) A plaster mould closes round the code, sealed Cast.

84
00:08:13.653 --> 00:08:18.253
(narrator) The notify line flashes red, and a big red stamp says NO!

85
00:08:18.453 --> 00:08:19.393
(narrator) on the test.

86
00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:25.700
♪ I'll make this more solid! I'll test every call! ♪

87
00:08:25.700 --> 00:08:32.090
♪ The order of helpers— I'll check it all! ♪

88
00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:39.180
♪ My implementation Is captured just so! ♪

89
00:08:39.490 --> 00:08:45.960
♪ If one small step changes, The tests will say "NO!" ♪

90
00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:50.733
[music]

91
00:08:50.733 --> 00:09:02.153
(narrator) The Test Engineer glides in from the right, with a woman's face of her own, a white lab coat over a lilac shirt, pens in the pocket, goggles round the neck, and a magnifying glass.

92
00:09:02.353 --> 00:09:04.433
(narrator) The Teammate freezes, sweating.

93
00:09:04.633 --> 00:09:10.913
(narrator) In the code, two helpers are merged into one, and a last line says Sam now holds the seat.

94
00:09:11.113 --> 00:09:24.393
(narrator) But the test card cracks and turns red, stamped Failed; the second build-status lamp turns red with a cross, his cheeks blush a little more, night falls in the window, and the clock runs to two.

95
00:09:24.593 --> 00:09:30.753
(narrator) The failing-test picture ejects a short, already crumpled report marked with large red crosses.

96
00:09:30.953 --> 00:09:34.633
(narrator) It flies onto the left desk and lands hanging over the edge.

97
00:09:34.833 --> 00:09:46.293
(narrator) A sticker says Test the behavior, and the test rewrites itself to check that Sam has the seat and the previous owner no longer has it; it passes, but the discarded report remains.

98
00:09:46.493 --> 00:09:56.373
(narrator) Then four little old test cards with faces cling to the bottom of the code with hearts, and the Test Engineer tugs at the code while the tests haul it back and shriek.

99
00:09:56.573 --> 00:10:03.693
(narrator) A brief label above their head identifies the Test Engineer; a matching magnifier sticker stays attached to their lab coat.

100
00:10:04.287 --> 00:10:08.247
♪ (Test Engineer) I merged two small helpers; The transfer worked right. ♪

101
00:10:08.247 --> 00:10:12.227
♪ Your brittle tests failed— There goes my whole night! ♪

102
00:10:12.227 --> 00:10:17.027
♪ Test what it does, not how: Then my friend's ticket should show! ♪

103
00:10:17.027 --> 00:10:22.127
♪ They're in love with your old code And won't let it go! ♪

104
00:10:22.833 --> 00:10:25.133
(narrator) Act four, Information hiding.

105
00:10:25.333 --> 00:10:30.133
(narrator) The Shows module is a little building with two doors, which he shoves wide open.

106
00:10:30.333 --> 00:10:35.333
(narrator) Inside is its table of shows, each with a showtime, like Friday 8 PM.

107
00:10:35.533 --> 00:10:46.813
(narrator) Three pages stand at the right as little houses, Tickets, Reminders, and Check-in; each reaches a long arm straight into the times, and each gets its own time math on a tag.

108
00:10:47.013 --> 00:10:53.393
(narrator) Then he switches the stored times to UTC, and the times change to Saturday 3 AM, and so on.

109
00:10:53.973 --> 00:10:56.833
♪ (Teammate) Information hiding? What's there to conceal? ♪

110
00:10:56.833 --> 00:11:00.213
♪ Each page reads the showtime Its own way! Ideal! ♪

111
00:11:00.213 --> 00:11:03.553
♪ Let every page do the math! It's simple! Direct! ♪

112
00:11:03.553 --> 00:11:07.200
♪ I'll switch them to UTC— What could that affect? ♪

113
00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:20.920
(narrator) The UI Developer glides in from the right, with a woman's face of her own, a mustard turtleneck under a denim painter's apron with dabs of paint and a brush in its pocket, rust trousers, and a palette in their right hand.

114
00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:23.200
(narrator) The Teammate freezes, sweating.

115
00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:27.440
(narrator) Tickets, Reminders, and Check-in crack, each with a red cross.

116
00:11:27.640 --> 00:11:32.080
(narrator) The third build-status lamp fails, and his cheeks blush a little more.

117
00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:43.820
(narrator) The times glow red, and dashed red lines race out to eight chips: receipts, emails, exports, reports, calendar, search, countdown, and scanner.

118
00:11:44.020 --> 00:11:51.720
(narrator) The dependency picture stays visible through the advice about hiding choices in one module, while bandages go on the affected clients.

119
00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:56.560
(narrator) The UI Developer's hair turns silver and a cane appears in their left hand.

120
00:11:56.760 --> 00:12:02.680
(narrator) After overnight, sunrise slowly brightens the window as the clock reaches 9:20 a.m.

121
00:12:02.880 --> 00:12:09.000
(narrator) On overnight, they swing their right hand and throw the paint palette they are holding against the far right wall.

122
00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:15.700
(narrator) The palette flies continuously from the emptying hand to the wall, where its paint splashes and stays.

123
00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:20.320
(narrator) The paint trails slowly creep farther down the wall through the rest of the song.

124
00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:24.820
(narrator) The wooden palette rebounds, falls, and settles on the floor below.

125
00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:31.740
(narrator) A brief label above their head identifies the UI Developer; a matching pencil sticker stays attached to their apron.

126
00:12:32.340 --> 00:12:35.860
♪ (UI Developer) Now tickets, reminders, And check-in need repair! ♪

127
00:12:35.860 --> 00:12:39.430
♪ One detail has changed— It's used EVERYWHERE! ♪

128
00:12:39.430 --> 00:12:42.840
♪ Hide choices that change In one module— right? ♪

129
00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:46.767
♪ You moved every showtime— I've aged overnight! ♪

130
00:12:47.067 --> 00:12:47.887
(narrator) Refrain.

131
00:12:48.087 --> 00:13:01.879
(narrator) Again the Teammate lifts his laptop high on one palm in a golden light while the build status board retains three red failed checks, sets it back to show his code in a gilded frame, and blows a chef's kiss.

132
00:13:02.079 --> 00:13:06.459
(narrator) This time the Tiny fixes counter goes from 1 to 26.

133
00:13:06.659 --> 00:13:09.939
(narrator) His laptop's six lamps and the footlights turn green.

134
00:13:10.540 --> 00:13:13.500
♪ (Teammate) It works on my machine! ♪

135
00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:17.780
♪ The finest code you've seen! ♪

136
00:13:17.780 --> 00:13:20.080
♪ One tiny fix— Or twenty-six— ♪

137
00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:23.700
♪ And all the lights turn green! ♪

138
00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:26.140
(narrator) Act five, Shared history.

139
00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:33.120
(narrator) A commit graph shows shared main: an old base commit and four teammates' commits, each labelled fix.

140
00:13:33.320 --> 00:13:41.200
(narrator) Below it runs his wiggly branch of six small commits: wip, oops, fix, typo, wip2, and eh.

141
00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:43.660
(narrator) They fold into one tidy commit.

142
00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:51.000
(narrator) He force-pushes it onto main, knocking the four fix commits off the line, under a stamp: Force push.

143
00:13:51.200 --> 00:14:02.020
(narrator) He sweeps the fallen commits away with a broom; the earlier clipboard in the bin, fallen old transfers package, failed-test report, and paint splatter with its fallen palette remain.

144
00:14:02.220 --> 00:14:07.260
(narrator) A card says Code review: 1 commit to read, with a sticker, Easy!

145
00:14:07.857 --> 00:14:11.167
♪ I'll squash all my changes To one tidy line! ♪

146
00:14:11.167 --> 00:14:14.207
♪ Then force-push to shared main— That history will shine! ♪

147
00:14:14.207 --> 00:14:17.727
♪ One clean line of progress! Less clutter to see! ♪

148
00:14:17.727 --> 00:14:23.067
♪ Review will be easy— That's teamwork from me! ♪

149
00:14:23.367 --> 00:14:33.627
(narrator) The Back-End Developer glides in from the right, with a woman's face of her own, a charcoal hoodie with a little database on the chest, black jeans, sneakers, and a coffee mug.

150
00:14:33.827 --> 00:14:35.907
(narrator) The Teammate freezes, sweating.

151
00:14:36.107 --> 00:14:38.867
(narrator) Dashed outlines show where their four commits were.

152
00:14:39.067 --> 00:14:45.927
(narrator) The old base commit gets cobwebs and a tag, old; the fourth build-status lamp turns red with a cross.

153
00:14:46.127 --> 00:14:49.867
(narrator) A stamp reads Broken, and his cheeks blush a little more.

154
00:14:50.067 --> 00:14:53.927
(narrator) A red card says Never force-push, then Merge first.

155
00:14:54.127 --> 00:15:03.907
(narrator) All four fix commits drop back onto main after his, dawn breaks, the clock turns to six, and the four failed build checks remain red.

156
00:15:04.107 --> 00:15:11.727
(narrator) The Back-End Developer points at him; two calendar cards, Saturday and Sunday, both free, drift up and vanish.

157
00:15:11.927 --> 00:15:20.927
(narrator) The Back-End Developer winds up, then on the last word, gone, throws the mug hard from their left hand toward the rear wall beside the clock.

158
00:15:21.127 --> 00:15:30.147
(narrator) Coffee splashes at the high impact, leaving a stain beside the still-visible clock, with two drips that slowly lengthen downward for the rest of the song.

159
00:15:30.347 --> 00:15:38.087
(narrator) The mug breaks; a few pieces fall and settle farther back on the floor between the bin and the fallen package, beside a wet patch.

160
00:15:38.287 --> 00:15:42.367
(narrator) The empty hand follows through, and the stain and fragments remain.

161
00:15:42.567 --> 00:15:49.667
(narrator) A brief label above their head identifies the Back-End Developer; a matching database sticker stays attached to their hoodie.

162
00:15:50.247 --> 00:15:53.567
♪ (Back-End Developer) I pushed all our fixes! They're gone from shared main! ♪

163
00:15:53.567 --> 00:15:56.627
♪ You forced your OLD history— We're broken again! ♪

164
00:15:56.627 --> 00:16:00.307
♪ Never force-push! Merge first! I'll restore them by dawn. ♪

165
00:16:00.307 --> 00:16:04.867
♪ Some sprint that you saved us! My weekend is gone! ♪

166
00:16:05.167 --> 00:16:07.007
(narrator) Act six, Secrets.

167
00:16:07.207 --> 00:16:16.547
(narrator) The coffee stain and broken mug remain with the earlier clipboard in the bin, old transfers package on the floor, report, and paint splatter with its fallen palette.

168
00:16:16.747 --> 00:16:20.347
(narrator) Night falls, and the clock spins to a quarter to midnight.

169
00:16:20.547 --> 00:16:36.347
(narrator) He pats his chest and sends the Back-End Developer to bed: a bed appears behind them, they yawn, lie down in it under a purple checked quilt, and pull a sleep mask over their eyes, with Z's drifting up; then the bed glides off to the right with them asleep in it.

170
00:16:36.547 --> 00:16:45.247
(narrator) A dark card shows the key in .env, and in app.py the hard-coded key is struck out for one read from the environment.

171
00:16:45.447 --> 00:16:48.327
(narrator) A sticker says Safer!, and he bows.

172
00:16:48.527 --> 00:17:05.067
(narrator) Then a folder, my-app, holds app.py, a readme, and .env with a key; with git add, all three fly into a commit box, and with git push the box flies up into a cloud marked Public repo, where three keys hang.

173
00:17:05.267 --> 00:17:10.567
(narrator) Gold keys rain down from the cloud into the audience, whose hands reach up for them.

174
00:17:11.147 --> 00:17:14.497
♪ (Teammate) I'll take the deployment! You get some sleep! ♪

175
00:17:14.497 --> 00:17:17.547
♪ The keys live in dot-env, Not hard-coded now! ♪

176
00:17:17.547 --> 00:17:20.827
♪ A safer deployment— Allow me to bow! ♪

177
00:17:20.827 --> 00:17:24.287
♪ I add the whole folder; One push, and we're done! ♪

178
00:17:24.287 --> 00:17:29.500
♪ At last, a smooth handoff For everyone! ♪

179
00:17:29.800 --> 00:17:43.160
(narrator) The Security Engineer glides in from the right, with a woman's face of her own, a black tactical jacket with chest pockets, epaulettes, a gold shield badge, and an earpiece wire, black cargo trousers, and boots.

180
00:17:43.360 --> 00:17:45.440
(narrator) The Teammate freezes, sweating.

181
00:17:45.640 --> 00:17:53.860
(narrator) A card shows .gitignore without .env, and a red card shows Commit 4f2a, with the keys in it.

182
00:17:54.060 --> 00:17:58.800
(narrator) His cheeks blush another shade, and the fifth build-status lamp fails.

183
00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:01.940
(narrator) Two masked burglars grab the keys from the cloud.

184
00:18:02.140 --> 00:18:11.680
(narrator) Three server racks spin up, the board keeps its five failed checks, and a cloud bill climbs to minus 48,200 dollars, in the red.

185
00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:15.540
(narrator) A rocket marked Theirs, on bags of money, launches.

186
00:18:15.740 --> 00:18:28.320
(narrator) Exhaust from its nozzle scorches a broad vertical path up the upper wall, leaving a much larger mark than the earlier damage: a textured dark centre, warm rim, and soft soot edges.

187
00:18:28.520 --> 00:18:33.040
(narrator) Small muted amber flames appear within the scorch once the rocket clears it.

188
00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:41.220
(narrator) Their tips curl and flicker, and the flames keep growing and spreading over more of the wall until the curtain finishes falling.

189
00:18:41.420 --> 00:18:43.880
(narrator) The clock and status board remain visible.

190
00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:53.200
(narrator) The Teammate adds .env to .gitignore, but the commit is still there, and he groans as five copies, each with a key, pop up.

191
00:18:53.400 --> 00:18:59.980
(narrator) The Security Engineer stamps their keys Rotated, and he points, aghast, as the bill climbs again.

192
00:19:00.180 --> 00:19:07.100
(narrator) A brief label above their head identifies the Security Engineer; a matching shield sticker stays attached to their jacket.

193
00:19:07.667 --> 00:19:10.947
♪ (Security Engineer) Dot-env's not in gitignore— You committed the keys! ♪

194
00:19:10.947 --> 00:19:14.067
♪ Our PUBLIC repo hands them To strangers with ease! ♪

195
00:19:14.067 --> 00:19:17.567
♪ Thieves are spinning up servers; We're deep in the red! ♪

196
00:19:17.567 --> 00:19:21.307
♪ You wanted a launch— We're funding THEIRS instead! ♪

197
00:19:21.307 --> 00:19:22.907
♪ (Teammate) I'll add it to gitignore! ♪

198
00:19:22.907 --> 00:19:24.187
♪ (Security Engineer) That won't uncommit it! ♪

199
00:19:24.187 --> 00:19:25.307
♪ (Teammate) Old copies remain! ♪

200
00:19:25.307 --> 00:19:26.807
♪ (Security Engineer) Rotate those credentials— ♪

201
00:19:26.807 --> 00:19:28.100
♪ (Teammate) They're spending again! ♪

202
00:19:28.400 --> 00:19:29.100
(narrator) Finale.

203
00:19:29.300 --> 00:19:48.240
(narrator) The room keeps all six traces: the discarded user-story clipboard angled in the bin, the fallen old transfers package, the crumpled report on the desk, the paint splatter on the wall with its fallen palette, the coffee stain and mug pieces, and the large rocket scorch with its slowly growing flames.

204
00:19:48.440 --> 00:19:56.220
(narrator) All six teammates come back empty-handed as a chorus line behind him, hands on hips; the UI Developer is still silver-haired.

205
00:19:56.420 --> 00:20:01.320
(narrator) He lifts his laptop high on one palm once more, then sets it back on the desk.

206
00:20:01.520 --> 00:20:08.480
(narrator) The Tiny fixes counter jumps from 1 to 96, and the six teammates facepalm, one after another.

207
00:20:08.680 --> 00:20:26.340
(narrator) His laptop's lamps and the footlights turn green, confetti bursts, and he holds the last note, arms high, while the teammates lower their hands and watch curiously, with lifted brows and attentive eyes, and the build status board retains five red failed checks.

208
00:20:26.540 --> 00:20:31.980
(narrator) During the held note, his laptop changes to CI tests running, with waiting dots.

209
00:20:32.180 --> 00:20:35.420
(narrator) All six board lamps go dark and show ellipses.

210
00:20:35.620 --> 00:20:46.800
(narrator) Three seconds later, a second before he says Strange, the screen reads CI Build failed under a red cross, and all six board lamps pulse red with solid crosses.

211
00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:59.400
(narrator) The silent teammates react first with startled dismay at the failed build, then disbelief at Strange; their brows knit and their mouths tighten in anger as he repeats It worked on my machine.

212
00:20:59.600 --> 00:21:08.300
(narrator) Compact role reminders reappear directly above the returning cast’s heads, then fade before the laptop rises; their costume stickers remain.

213
00:21:08.867 --> 00:21:12.247
♪ It works on my machine! ♪

214
00:21:12.247 --> 00:21:16.327
♪ The finest code you've seen! ♪

215
00:21:16.327 --> 00:21:19.647
♪ One tiny fix— Or ninety-six— ♪

216
00:21:19.647 --> 00:21:28.433
♪ And all the lights turn green! ♪

217
00:21:28.733 --> 00:21:29.393
(narrator) Outro.

218
00:21:29.593 --> 00:21:30.913
(narrator) The music stops.

219
00:21:31.113 --> 00:21:34.073
(narrator) All six traces of the setbacks remain in the room.

220
00:21:34.273 --> 00:21:40.173
(narrator) His laptop shows a red cross and CI Build failed, which appeared one second before Strange.

221
00:21:40.373 --> 00:21:44.253
(narrator) All six build-board lamps pulse red with solid crosses.

222
00:21:44.453 --> 00:21:48.773
(narrator) His cheeks are subtly flushed; he frowns at the laptop, puzzled.

223
00:21:48.973 --> 00:21:52.813
(narrator) He steps to the desk and reaches for the keyboard with a sly smile.

224
00:21:53.013 --> 00:22:09.973
(narrator) Then all six teammates sing together: your hero, the worried teammate from the overture, in a blue hoodie with a gold star, glides in from the left between him and the desk, palm out, the six in the line-up hold out their palms too, and he jumps back with both hands up.

225
00:22:10.173 --> 00:22:17.553
(narrator) On the closing chord the curtain falls, and the moral of the opera is posted on it: Ask your users what they need.

226
00:22:17.753 --> 00:22:19.453
(narrator) Pin your dependency versions.

227
00:22:19.653 --> 00:22:22.093
(narrator) Test behavior, not implementation.

228
00:22:22.293 --> 00:22:24.313
(narrator) Hide decisions likely to change.

229
00:22:24.513 --> 00:22:26.753
(narrator) Never force-push a shared branch.

230
00:22:26.953 --> 00:22:28.573
(narrator) Keep secrets out of Git.

231
00:22:29.193 --> 00:22:33.413
Strange. It worked on my machine.

232
00:22:33.413 --> 00:22:36.673
I'll just make one small change.

233
00:22:36.673 --> 00:22:43.003
(All six teammates) Step away from the keyboard! Step away from the keyboard!

234
00:22:43.003 --> 00:22:46.333
[music]
